type — Creates type bindings; runtime type values use reflect.Type.

Syntax:
  type UserId = string
  type Result = string | int
  type MaybeInt = int?
  interface Iterator {
    type Item
    type Error extends string = string
  }
  class IntIterator {
    implements Iterator {
      type Item = int
    }
  }
  function from_runtime(t: reflect.Type) -> reflect.Type {
    let type T = t;
    reflect.Type.of<T>()
  }

At top level, `type Name = ...` creates an alias. Inside an interface, `type Item`, `type Item extends Bound`, `type Item = Default`, and `type Item extends Bound = Default` declare associated types. Inside an `implements` block, bind associated types with `type Item = Concrete`. Inside a function, lambda, or block body, `let type T = t;` binds a block-scoped type parameter to a runtime type value (`t: reflect.Type | reflect.TypeView`, or a builder's pending type; an `unknown` must be narrowed first with `match` or `is`). `T` is a rigid type parameter for the rest of the block: nothing typed by `T` may leave the block except through a type that does not mention it, such as `unknown`. `let type T extends I = t else { ... };` also gives `T` the interface bound `I` (several bounds join with `&`): the statement checks the bound when it runs and runs the diverging `else { ... }` block when the type misses it. A bounded `let type` must have that `else` block. A body-level `type T = ...` alias and `unreflect(...)` no longer exist; write `let type`. The old `type.of<T>()` shorthand and bare `type` value annotation do not exist either; write `reflect.Type.of<T>()` and `reflect.Type`.
